<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amartya Sen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. <a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Amartya Kumar Sen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amartya Sen is an Indian economist and philosopher, born in 1933 in Santiniketan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, and named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>. He received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Economic Sciences in 1998 for his contributions to welfare economics, and has also worked on social choice theory, development economics and the measures of well-being of countries. In Poverty and Famines (1981) he argued that famine comes not only from a lack of food but from inequalities in the mechanisms for distributing it. He has taught in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> since 1972, was master of Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and received the Bharat Ratna in 1999.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amartya-sen.jpg" alt="Amartya Sen" width="330" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elke Wetzig (Elya); derivative work by ChaudhryAzan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amartya_Sen_20071128_cologne_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mike Davis blamed colonial policy for the famines that killed millions in British India in the late 19th century, and <b>Amartya Sen</b> showed that the Bengal famine of 1943 struck without any great fall in food supply.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>The economist <b>Amartya Sen</b> observed that no substantial famine has ever occurred in a democracy with a relatively free press.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>The Soviet Union industrialised rapidly and achieved near-universal literacy, and the economist Michael Ellman notes that communist states compared well with Western states on some health indicators, such as infant mortality and life expectancy; <b>Amartya Sen</b> observed that several made large gains in life expectancy.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Strengths</a></li></ul></section>
